From: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: Thu, 18 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] client/wayland: Preserve depressed modifiers in
 reset_modifiers

ibus_wayland_im_reset_modifiers() is called from
_create_input_context_done(), which runs on every input-method
activation, i.e. on every focus change to a surface whose text input
enables.  Its purpose is to clear composition state (virtual latches,
ISO Level3/5, dead keys), but it implemented the clear by invoking
input_method_keyboard_modifiers() with mods_depressed = 0.

The modifiers handler cannot distinguish that internal call from a
protocol event: xkb_state_update_mask() writes the zeros into
key_user.state and key_sys.state and priv->modifiers is rebuilt from
zero, erasing every physically held modifier until the next modifier
transition event arrives.  Sway sends no such event because, from the
compositor's seat state, nothing changed - the modifier has been
continuously down.

Effect (deterministic, verified with ~/.cache/ibus/wayland.log):

  Update modifiers serial:2029 depress:4 ... orig_depre:4   <- Ctrl down
  Update modifiers serial:0    depress:0 ... orig_depre:0   <- this reset

Repro: hold Ctrl, press N (new window opens), press V -> the letter "v"
is committed instead of pasting.  Any modifier transition (e.g. tapping
Shift while still holding Ctrl) restores the state and the next Ctrl+V
pastes, which is how the erase was isolated.

Fix: pass the currently depressed mask of key_sys.state instead of 0.
The direct composition-state assignments and the latched/locked
handling are unchanged, so the reset still clears what it was meant to
clear; only physically held modifiers survive the context recreation.
The engine-switch caller (ibus_bus_get_global_engine path) benefits
identically.

Serial-0 invocations do not forward to the virtual keyboard
(input_method_keyboard_modifiers() returns early for key_serial == 0),
so this change does not alter what the compositor or the focused
client are told; it only stops IBus from discarding its own modifier
state.
---
 client/wayland/ibuswaylandim.c | 13 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/client/wayland/ibuswaylandim.c b/client/wayland/ibuswaylandim.c
--- a/client/wayland/ibuswaylandim.c
+++ b/client/wayland/ibuswaylandim.c
@@ -514,6 +514,7 @@
 ibus_wayland_im_reset_modifiers (IBusWaylandIM *wlim)
 {
     IBusWaylandIMPrivate *priv;
+    xkb_mod_mask_t mods_depressed = 0;
     xkb_mod_mask_t mods_locked = 0;
     xkb_layout_index_t  group = 0;
 
@@ -526,11 +527,21 @@
     priv->pressed_dead_key = 0;
     priv->released_dead_key_wo_press = 0;
     if (priv->key_user.state && priv->key_sys.state) {
+        /* Preserve physically held modifiers across focus changes:
+         * _create_input_context_done() calls this on every activation
+         * and zeroing the depressed mods here erased a modifier held
+         * across the window switch until the next modifier transition
+         * event arrived (e.g. Ctrl+N, new window, Ctrl+V => "v").
+         * Composition state (latches, ISO level3/5, dead keys) is
+         * still cleared above and latched mods are still reset below.
+         */
+        mods_depressed = xkb_state_serialize_mods (priv->key_sys.state,
+                                                   XKB_STATE_MODS_DEPRESSED);
         mods_locked = xkb_state_serialize_mods (priv->key_sys.state,
                                                 XKB_STATE_LOCKED);
         group = xkb_state_serialize_layout (priv->key_sys.state,
                                             XKB_STATE_LAYOUT_LOCKED);
-        input_method_keyboard_modifiers (wlim, NULL, 0, 0, 0,
+        input_method_keyboard_modifiers (wlim, NULL, 0, mods_depressed, 0,
                                          mods_locked,
                                          group);
     } else {
